PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C. None of the above is correct.

The collective results are unambiguous: at the matched 10 mg/kg dose used for anti-TNF versus ADC, the ADC produces clearly superior inflammation control (dose-dependent ear-swelling reduction in Exp 1; rapid paw-swelling reversal by day 7 in Exp 2) and substantially less bone-density loss (–0.3 vs –0.75 mm³ at day 14 in Exp 3). GRM alone is also anti-inflammatory.  

Option E claims the chosen doses were “correct” for comparing efficiency and side effects of anti-TNF and ADC. While the two agents were dosed identically, the overall experimental design deliberately tested GRM at only 3 mg/kg in the bone-density arm, which prevents any complete, head-to-head evaluation across all three compounds. That mismatch directly undermines the assertion that the dosing scheme was optimally or correctly chosen for the full comparison the study set out to perform. Every other listed option likewise contains at least one factual contradiction with the reported numbers (inverted efficacy, false equal-risk claims, or unsupported GRM extrapolations). Consequently only C remains consistent with the full experimental record.}

